WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.710
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We should aim
to reduce toil via automation

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:03.480
whenever possible.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.880
Today, I want to show you
how to automate the creation

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.510
of monitoring dashboards.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:09.296
This is The Stack Doctor.

00:00:09.296 --> 00:00:13.104
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.650
While dashboards are
not a replacement

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:21.070
for well-defined SLOs,
alerting, or good observability,

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:22.960
they're widely used
by many involved

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.182
in service reliability.

00:00:24.182 --> 00:00:25.640
And they are a
must-have capability

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:26.868
in any monitoring setup.

00:00:26.868 --> 00:00:29.410
The first question you should
ask when setting up a dashboard

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.300
is, what should be
on the dashboard?

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.750
Thankfully, the answer to
that has been well documented

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.130
in the monitoring distributed
systems chapter in the SRE

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:38.870
book.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.610
If you have nothing else,
start with the golden signals--

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.550
traffic, errors,
latency, and saturation.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.380
I'll go over each one.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:49.140
Let's take the
services running in GKE

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.320
and managed by the Istio
service mesh as an example.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.880
We've covered using
Istio with Cloud

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.463
Monitoring and the
excellent telemetry

00:00:55.463 --> 00:00:56.910
it provides in the past.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.560
Today, we're going
to focus on using

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.110
some of the key metrics it
provides about traffic, errors,

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.790
and latency for our service.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.400
Here's an example dashboard
showing all three.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.250
The first golden signal is
traffic, essentially a measure

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.010
of how much user activity
the service is responding to.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.750
Thankfully, Istio
provides this natively

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.640
for every service in the mesh.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.790
The metric is called
Server Request Count.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.420
And the first chart
in my dashboard,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.910
request rates by service,
users that metric

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.880
and groups the results by the
destination service name label.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.680
The next chart,
errors by service,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.837
uses the same metric
and grouping options.

00:01:28.837 --> 00:01:30.670
The only difference is
that the data is then

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:34.090
filtered to only count requests
where the response code is not

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.320
200.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.910
Note that this is
a rough approach,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.960
as it does include about 300
redirects and 400 errors, which

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.150
are often the result of
misconfigured or misbehaving

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.170
clients.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.690
The last chart, again,
uses an Istio metric.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:49.510
This time it's Server
Responsive Latencies, grouped

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:52.810
by destination_service_name
and using the 99th percentile

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:53.860
aggregation.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.690
I'm not filtering out
errors for this one,

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:56.920
though it might be
a good idea if you

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.420
want to better understand how
long it takes to return errors

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.233
and successes separately.

00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:02.650
Creating such a
dashboard manually

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.900
is not difficult, assuming
that the data is available.

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.980
But it is toil that should
be automated if possible.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:11.410
With the release of the
dashboard's API, it now is.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.210
You can also use the
API to, for example,

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.950
copy dashboards
between workspaces

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.878
to create additional
standardization via automation.

00:02:17.878 --> 00:02:20.170
Let's take a look into how
we can use the API to create

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.480
this dashboard.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.370
The general idea is
pretty straightforward.

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.860
We simply call the
projects.dashboards.create

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.290
method, passing a
dashboard's object, which

00:02:29.290 --> 00:02:32.440
contains a name, description,
and a set of widget objects

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:36.220
that specify the
charts themselves.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.540
Here's the JSON definition
for the Request Rates chart.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.040
We'll put the links
to the blog post

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.940
where you can see
the details below.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.560
I created this
manually, but you can

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.570
use the API Explorer to
create your own baseline

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:48.390
and simply populate
it with parameters.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.508
Note that the filter specifies
the resource and metric.

00:02:51.508 --> 00:02:53.050
This is also where
you would specify,

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:54.900
for example, the cluster
name, service name,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.120
or other attributes if needed.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.970
I use the default aligner
and the sum reducer

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.950
to simply total up the
requests and group them

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:04.860
by the destination_service_name
metric label.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.660
To create the
dashboard itself, we

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.760
specified it as a grid
layout with the number

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.490
of columns we want and then
define the charts as widgets.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:15.360
We then pass this in the body
of the request to the API.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.380
And here we are.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:19.960
Our dashboard is created
and ready to use.

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.900
We're working on the features to
make the API even more useful,

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:25.240
including enabling through
the G Cloud command line.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:27.460
Also, contributors are
discussing and planning

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.860
the terraform module for
the monitoring dashboard API

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.000
in GitHub.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:33.520
But you can and should
get started with dashboard

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.027
automation today.

00:03:35.027 --> 00:03:35.860
Thanks for watching.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:37.030
Come back next time.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.170
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:39.490
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:43.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

